<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  AC one arm drive/passive parameters — SYSID set.

  The identified set from an offline system-identification run
  against ARX Real hardware, taken from its accepted model.
    damping / friction / armature  <- each <joint> element
    drive_stiffness                <- actuator gainprm
    drive_damping                  <- -1x biasprm[2]
                                      (biasprm="0 -Kp -Kd", biastype="affine")
  Schema mirrors AC1.params.default.xacro so a diff of the two files
  reads as a pure spreadsheet of number changes.

  THE LEFT-ARM SET IS APPLIED TO BOTH ARMS.  The run produced two
  different per-side sets; those differences are an artifact of
  regularization, not a property of the hardware, so one side is
  carried on both.  The verbatim per-side output is preserved in
  robots/arx/arx_drive_params.sysid.yaml under
  `arx_acone_raw_run_output` (record only), alongside the armature
  values and command delay that URDF cannot express.

  WHY ONE SIDE ON BOTH:

    * The two arms are mechanically identical.  Every link mass, COM
      and inertia matches L to R, joint axes are identical, and the
      mounts differ only by y = +/-0.25 — a translation, not a mirror.
      Both arms see identical gravity loading in identical
      configurations.  Nothing in the plant justifies a split.

    * The free (unpenalized) fits are MORE symmetric than the
      penalized ones, on different joints.  Left free kp vs right
      free kp, each from the run that preceded its accepted fit:
          J1  1254 vs 10168   8.1x apart
          J2  1103 vs   940     15%
          J3   406 vs   350     14%
          J4   364 vs  6809  18.7x apart
          J5  1291 vs  1220    5.5%
          J6  38.5 vs  26.7     31%
      The soft-500 penalized values instead agree on J1-J4 and blow up
      on J5 (3.6x) and J6 (11.0x).  Adding the penalty moved the
      disagreement to different joints — the per-side kp differences
      are not stable under a change of regularization and so carry no
      information about the arms.

    * R6 kp is provably unidentified.  Same data, same normalization,
      towel excluded in both: the unpenalized fit converged to 26.74
      and the penalized one to 630.29.  A 23x spread, and
      in the direction the penalty cannot produce — it only pushes
      values down.  Two converged minima on one objective means the R6
      kp surface is flat; the run's 630.29 was basin selection.

    * The right-arm kd layer collapsed outright.  Its free fit
      returned R1 kd = 1.2e-29 and R2 kd = 5.0e-13 — a damping
      coefficient optimizing to 1e-29 has no measurable influence on
      the loss.  That layer was rejected, so the run's right arm never
      had a fitted kd at all; it kept baseline 5/5/5/2/2/2.  Only kp
      was ever fitted on that side.

    * The two sides were not fitted on the same data.  Left: 14 fit /
      9 validation episodes, towel INCLUDED.  Right: 9 / 6, towel
      EXCLUDED, and up to 2x less joint excitation (J1 sweep 0.74 rad
      vs 1.31; J5 0.57 vs 0.90).

  WHY THE LEFT SIDE: 1.5x the episodes, the towel task included, wider
  excitation, and the only side where all five families were fitted.
  Validation tracking is equal or better on every joint — J1-J4 within
  16%, while the right arm is 39% worse on J5 and 52% worse on J6.

  NOT symmetrized: the command delay, 0.085 s left and 0.089 s right.
  Delay was the dominant and best-identified effect in the whole run
  (~90% of the reported tracking gain), the two values were fitted
  independently and agree to 4.7%, so that split is a measurement
  rather than an artifact.  It lives in the overlays and in the YAML
  sidecar, and has no consumer in the engine.

  The identification ran against the same upstream ARX source model
  this package was adapted from (see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES at the
  package root) — link masses, COMs and joint origins match
  AC1.urdf.xacro exactly, so these parameters belong to this
  kinematics.  The harness did pad the joint limits by 0.01 rad and
  cap actuator force at 27 N·m (J1-J3) / 7 N·m (J4-J6), tighter than
  the <limit effort> values here (30/40/30/15/10/10); limits and
  efforts are left alone.  That cap is a CONDITION of the fit, not a
  rating: 27 and 7 are motor-module datasheet peak torques for a
  DM-J4340 plus DM-J4310 pairing, and this arm's J1-J3 are EC-A4310.
  Do not carry 27/7 forward as hardware limits.  The overlays and the
  YAML sidecar publish no effort limit at all — the vendor rating lives
  here, in <limit effort>, and the harness cap is kept as provenance
  only, verbatim in the sidecar's raw-run record.

  Remaining caveats:
    * The run's own report calls these "effective closed-loop
      position-response parameters, not unique rigid-body dynamics
      ground truth".  They were fitted inside a position loop where
      command delay, kp, kd, damping, frictionloss and armature all
      trade against each other, so individual numbers are not
      separately identified.
    * About 90% of the headline tracking improvement (+54% left /
      +58% right normalized position RMS) came from fixing the command
      delay alone (+50.5% / +54.0%).  kp supplies most of the rest.
      damping + frictionloss + armature + kd together are worth ~1.6%
      on the left arm and nothing on the right.  Adopting the gains
      below without the delay adopts the small half of the result.
    * j1 armature 0.241 is 24x the stock 0.01 and ~2x the heaviest
      engine class value; j3 damping 1.80 and j4 frictionloss 0.525
      are likewise at the edge of their search boxes.  Box-bound hits
      are a sign the loop was absorbing something else.
    * A later run isolated the right wrist's kd at 69.57 (34x
      baseline) for +47.45% on R6 and +16.28% overall validation,
      moving other joints by <=0.069%.  It converged in 49 evaluations
      and has not been accepted.  If it is, J6 would be the one
      joint with a defensible per-side kd — the place to revisit this
      file.

  Gripper joints are deliberately absent.  The run's accepted gripper
  gains (kp 2000, kd 200) already equal the engine's gripper class
  defaults in physics_params.yaml, so there is nothing to change.

  Runtime status: compatible bringup scenes also select robot.joint_overlays.
  Channel B preserves the identified per-joint values after class fallbacks
  in Isaac PhysX 6.2 and Newton MuJoCo-Warp. This xacro selector alone does
  not select runtime overlays; command delay remains unimplemented.

  Layout: left arm joint1..6 first, then right arm joint1..6, carrying
  the same values.

  Selection: `xacro ... param_source:=sysid`.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- joint1 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_l1_damping"         value="0.02646" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l1_friction"        value="0.00102394" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l1_drive_stiffness" value="515.513" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l1_drive_damping"   value="1.33108" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l1_armature"        value="0.241167" />

  <!-- joint2 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_l2_damping"         value="0.0238303" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l2_friction"        value="0.00102044" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l2_drive_stiffness" value="526.311" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l2_drive_damping"   value="2.97626" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l2_armature"        value="0.0453454" />

  <!-- joint3 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_l3_damping"         value="1.79972" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l3_friction"        value="0.0142011" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l3_drive_stiffness" value="507.589" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l3_drive_damping"   value="6.85319" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l3_armature"        value="0.0472377" />

  <!-- joint4 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_l4_damping"         value="0.0101474" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l4_friction"        value="0.525452" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l4_drive_stiffness" value="324.788" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l4_drive_damping"   value="1.0962" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l4_armature"        value="0.0148545" />

  <!-- joint5 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_l5_damping"         value="0.762774" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l5_friction"        value="0.292917" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l5_drive_stiffness" value="508.681" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l5_drive_damping"   value="4.60003" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l5_armature"        value="0.0979814" />

  <!-- joint6 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_l6_damping"         value="0.527118" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l6_friction"        value="0.278988" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l6_drive_stiffness" value="57.2908" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l6_drive_damping"   value="3.58765" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_l6_armature"        value="0.00416896" />

  <!-- joint1 -->
  <xacro:property name="acone_r1_damping"         value="0.02646" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_r1_friction"        value="0.00102394" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_r1_drive_stiffness" value="515.513" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_r1_drive_damping"   value="1.33108" />
  <xacro:property name="acone_r1_armature"        value="0.241167" />

